<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the outer Lucidos.app signature ONLY.

  NO DOUBLE HYPHEN ANYWHERE IN THIS COMMENT. codesign hands the file to
  AMFIUnserializeXML, whose XML parser rejects a "- -" pair inside a comment
  (strict XML forbids it) and dies with "syntax error near line 6". plutil
  lints the same file as OK, so the breakage is invisible until a signing run.
  Write flag names without their leading hyphens, or move the prose out of the
  file entirely.

  The release path signs with the hardened runtime, because notarization
  requires it and the hardened runtime denies a capture device to a binary that
  does not claim it, whatever Info.plist says. So each item needs BOTH halves:
  a usage string for the consent prompt's copy (Info.plist) and the matching
  entitlement here for the runtime.

  Two capture devices, one item each. The composer's Camera item takes a photo,
  paired with NSCameraUsageDescription. Its call toggle opens the microphone
  for the length of a call, paired with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription.

  Deliberately minimal. Lucidos is not sandboxed, so nothing here is a
  container permission; each key is a hardened-runtime capability we actually
  exercise. A key with no capture site behind it widens what the app may do for
  no reason, so add one only in the change that starts using it.

  THIS file is applied to the outer .app and to nothing else, and the bundle
  carries no nested bundles or XPC services for deep signing to propagate it to.
  sign_app_bundle also signs ~200 loose Mach-O files (the relocatable Postgres
  tree, the engine, the gateway, the CLI). None of them capture anything, so
  none of them claim a capture device.

  One of them does claim something else. The engine is a separate process, so
  this signature never governs it, and it carries its own
  EngineEntitlements.plist for the wasmtime code it compiles at run time. A
  bundled executable that needs a hardened-runtime capability gets its own file
  rather than a key here. See ADR 0160.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
